# MNIST — a conv net on Vulkan

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:nx_vulkan, path: Path.expand("../", __DIR__)},
  {:axon, "~> 0.7"},
  {:req, "~> 0.5"},
  {:kino, "~> 0.12"}
])
```

## What this notebook is

The usual MNIST convolutional classifier, with every op dispatching through
`Nx.Vulkan.VulkanoBackend` — conv included, as a native GPU shader (im2col +
GEMM) rather than a host fallback.

It also tells you the truth about where that helps and where it does not:

* **Inference is where this backend wins today** — measured **~130×** over
  `Nx.BinaryBackend` on a batch of 256 on an RTX 3060 Ti.
* **Training is correct but backward-bound** — ~1.2×, because Nx's conv
  *gradient* falls back to the host. The last section explains exactly why,
  and it is a fixable gap, not a law of nature.

Numbers here were measured on a 3060 Ti; a Kepler will be slower in absolute
terms but shows the same shape.

## 1. Get MNIST

Same source and layout as the [Axon MNIST
guide](https://axon.hexdocs.pm/mnist.html): IDX files from the CVDF mirror,
reshaped to `{n, 1, 28, 28}` (NCHW) and scaled to 0–1.

```elixir
base = "https://storage.googleapis.com/cvdf-datasets/mnist/"

fetch = fn name ->
  %{body: body} = Req.get!(base <> name, decode_body: false)
  :zlib.gunzip(body)
end

<<_::32, n_images::32, n_rows::32, n_cols::32, image_bytes::binary>> =
  fetch.("train-images-idx3-ubyte.gz")

<<_::32, _n_labels::32, label_bytes::binary>> = fetch.("train-labels-idx1-ubyte.gz")

{n_images, n_rows, n_cols}
```

```elixir
# Host-side tensors: keep the full corpus on BinaryBackend and move only the
# batches we actually use to the GPU.
images_all =
  image_bytes
  |> Nx.from_binary({:u, 8})
  |> Nx.reshape({n_images, 1, n_rows, n_cols})
  |> Nx.divide(255.0)
  |> Nx.as_type(:f32)

labels_all =
  label_bytes
  |> Nx.from_binary({:u, 8})
  |> Nx.new_axis(-1)
  |> Nx.equal(Nx.iota({1, 10}))
  |> Nx.as_type(:f32)

{Nx.shape(images_all), Nx.shape(labels_all)}
```

## 2. The model

Two strided convolutions, then a softmax head.

**Why strided convs instead of `max_pool`:** pooling is a host fallback in
*both* directions on this backend — `window_max` forward and
`window_scatter_max` backward — so each pool layer costs two GPU↔host round
trips per step. A stride-2 conv downsamples the same way and stays on the
device. Swap in `Axon.max_pool(kernel_size: 2, strides: 2, channels: :first)`
if you want to feel the difference.

**`channels: :first` is not optional here.** Axon defaults to `:last`, so an
NCHW `{n, 1, 28, 28}` input silently becomes height 1 — and a 3×3 valid conv
or a 2×2 pool over height 1 produces an empty tensor and a confusing crash
deep in Nx. Every conv/pool layer needs the option.

```elixir
model =
  Axon.input("input", shape: {nil, 1, 28, 28})
  |> Axon.conv(8, kernel_size: 3, strides: 2, padding: :same,
       activation: :relu, channels: :first)
  |> Axon.conv(16, kernel_size: 3, strides: 2, padding: :same,
       activation: :relu, channels: :first)
  |> Axon.flatten()
  |> Axon.dense(10, activation: :softmax)

Axon.Display.as_graph(model, Nx.template({1, 1, 28, 28}, :f32))
```

```elixir
vk = Nx.Vulkan.VulkanoBackend
{init_fn, predict_fn} = Axon.build(model)

init_params = init_fn.(%{"input" => Nx.template({32, 1, 28, 28}, :f32)}, Axon.ModelState.empty())

# Axon holds parameters in a ModelState; move every tensor in it to a backend.
move = fn state, backend ->
  %{state | data:
    Map.new(state.data, fn {layer, ps} ->
      {layer, Map.new(ps, fn {k, v} -> {k, Nx.backend_transfer(v, backend)} end)}
    end)}
end

params_vk = move.(init_params, vk)
:ok
```

## 3. Train

`Nx.Defn.grad` needs every tensor it differentiates to reach it as an
**argument**, not as a closure capture — a GPU tensor captured in the closure
raises `"a tensor captured as a closure inside the gradient function is on a
non-default backend"`. So the step function takes params, x and y and is
applied with `Nx.Defn.jit_apply/3`.

Be honest with yourself about the clock before running this: at batch 32 a
step is **~15 s** on a 3060 Ti (~18 s on `BinaryBackend`). The default below
is 12 steps — about three minutes — enough to watch the loss fall. Raise
`n_steps` if you want real accuracy and have the time.

```elixir
batch = 32
n_steps = 12
lr = 0.05

loss_fn = fn params, x, y ->
  Axon.Losses.categorical_cross_entropy(y, predict_fn.(params, %{"input" => x}),
    reduction: :mean)
end

step_fn = fn params, x, y ->
  Nx.Defn.value_and_grad(params, fn p -> loss_fn.(p, x, y) end)
end

sgd = fn params, grads ->
  %{params | data:
    Map.new(params.data, fn {layer, ps} ->
      g = grads.data[layer]
      {layer, Map.new(ps, fn {k, w} -> {k, Nx.subtract(w, Nx.multiply(g[k], lr))} end)}
    end)}
end

batch_at = fn i ->
  start = rem(i * batch, 1024)
  x = images_all |> Nx.slice_along_axis(start, batch, axis: 0) |> Nx.backend_transfer(vk)
  y = labels_all |> Nx.slice_along_axis(start, batch, axis: 0) |> Nx.backend_transfer(vk)
  {x, y}
end

{trained, history} =
  Enum.reduce(0..(n_steps - 1), {params_vk, []}, fn i, {params, hist} ->
    {x, y} = batch_at.(i)
    {us, {loss, grads}} =
      :timer.tc(fn ->
        Nx.Defn.jit_apply(step_fn, [params, x, y], compiler: Nx.Defn.Evaluator)
      end)

    l = Nx.to_number(loss)
    IO.puts("step #{i}  loss=#{Float.round(l, 4)}  #{round(us / 1000)} ms")
    {sgd.(params, grads), [l | hist]}
  end)

Enum.reverse(history)
```

The loss should fall from ~2.3 (chance, `ln 10`) as it goes. Run the cell
again to continue from `trained` if you want more steps.

## 4. Inference — where the GPU actually wins

Conv *forward* is a native shader, so a big batch of predictions is where this
backend earns its keep. Same parameters, same math, two backends.

```elixir
n_eval = 256

x_host = Nx.slice_along_axis(images_all, 0, n_eval, axis: 0)
y_host = Nx.slice_along_axis(labels_all, 0, n_eval, axis: 0)

params_host = move.(trained, Nx.BinaryBackend)
x_vk = Nx.backend_transfer(x_host, vk)

time = fn f -> {us, v} = :timer.tc(f); {us / 1000, v} end

{t_host, out_host} =
  time.(fn -> predict_fn.(params_host, %{"input" => x_host}) end)

# one warm-up pass so shader compilation is not counted
_ = predict_fn.(trained, %{"input" => x_vk})

{t_vk, out_vk} =
  time.(fn ->
    predict_fn.(trained, %{"input" => x_vk}) |> Nx.backend_transfer(Nx.BinaryBackend)
  end)

%{
  binary_ms: Float.round(t_host, 1),
  vulkan_ms: Float.round(t_vk, 1),
  speedup: Float.round(t_host / t_vk, 1),
  max_abs_diff: out_host |> Nx.subtract(out_vk) |> Nx.abs() |> Nx.reduce_max() |> Nx.to_number()
}
```

Two things to read off that map: the speedup, and `max_abs_diff` — the two
backends agree to f32 rounding, so the speed is not bought with accuracy.

```elixir
predicted = out_vk |> Nx.argmax(axis: 1)
actual = y_host |> Nx.argmax(axis: 1)

accuracy =
  Nx.equal(predicted, actual) |> Nx.mean() |> Nx.to_number()

%{accuracy: accuracy, first_10_predicted: Nx.to_flat_list(predicted[0..9]),
  first_10_actual: Nx.to_flat_list(actual[0..9])}
```

With only a dozen training steps this will be well short of the ~99% a fully
trained MNIST CNN reaches — the point here is that the forward pass is fast
and correct, not that twelve steps is enough training.

## 5. Why training is slower than inference

Both passes run the same convolution, but only one of them runs on the GPU.

`Nx.Vulkan.VulkanoBackend.conv/4` dispatches to the native im2col + GEMM
shaders when the convolution is in the layout those shaders expect — among
other things, identity permutations for input, kernel, and output
(`conv_gpu_ok?/4`). A forward conv satisfies that.

Nx's conv *gradient* does not. It builds its backward convolutions with

```
conv_spec_transpose([dim0, dim1 | rest]) -> [dim1, dim0 | rest]
```

swapping the first two axes, so every backward conv carries a **non-identity
permutation**, fails the check, and falls back to `Nx.BinaryBackend.conv` —
pure Elixir, on the CPU. That single fallback is most of the ~15 s step, and
it is why training measures ~1.2× while inference measures ~130×.

Closing it means teaching the GPU path to transpose into its native layout
instead of refusing the work. Until then the useful shape of this backend is:
**train where you have EXLA, infer wherever you have Vulkan** — which is
rather the point of a backend that runs on GPUs CUDA never shipped for.
